# Focused source-boundary dispositions. No source/contract mutations.
D={}
def add(id,subject,domain,result,choice,**cases):D[id]=dict(subject=subject,domain=domain,result=result,choice=choice,cases=cases)
# FFT/chirp procedures and algebra.
add('alg-na-certified-unit-circle-phase','Certified rational-angle phase evaluation','an exact rational q and positive tolerance at most one quarter, finite precision and series caps','an enclosure of exp(pi i q) or an explicit inconclusive/range status','Lower interval endpoints fix the returned complex value; increasing integer precision and the finite series cap prescribe every call.',empty='N:An exact rational angle and a nonempty pair of sine/cosine enclosure coordinates are required; an absent input is not an angle.',one='N:There is no transform-length parameter: the object evaluated is one rational-angle phase, not a size-one vector family.',zero='C:At rational angle zero the exact target is one; the interval enclosure and return-width test still apply, and success is not promised merely from the angle being zero.',degenerate='C:Nonfinite enclosure endpoints, overflow or exhausted precision return the explicit failure/inconclusive branch rather than a phase-error certificate.',endpoints='C:The upper tolerance one quarter is admitted, and a width equal to the tolerance passes the non-strict epsilon-at-most-tau return test.')
add('alg-na-computed-fast-transforms-and-convolution','The unnormalised arbitrary-length transform dispatcher','a positive integer length, complex vector and finite phase/recursion controls','length-one return, radix-two recursion, least-divisor mixed radix or prime Bluestein output with propagated failure','The least divisor, least qualifying power of two, fixed strided indices and prescribed phase calls make the branch schedule deterministic.',one='C:The first instruction returns x_0 when N is one; it performs neither a factor search nor a butterfly.',zero='C:Zero vector entries are admissible; successful products, additions and scalings preserve their zero values, while phase/recursion failures still propagate.',degenerate='C:Failed phase enclosures, arithmetic range errors and insufficient radix-two depth retain their explicit statuses; no mixed-radix stability claim follows.',endpoints='C:Composite outputs use k_2+s k_1 with both indices in their full finite ranges; prime outputs retain exactly the first N padded-convolution entries.')
add('alg-na-computed-fft-cyclic-and-linear-convolution','Computed cyclic/linear convolution by unitary FFTs','positive linear lengths or a common positive power-of-two cyclic length','two forward transforms, pointwise products, inverse transform and square-root length scaling','Least power-of-two padding fixes the storage length; transform direction and all product/scaling orders are supplied.',empty='N:Linear input lengths are explicitly at least one; cyclic length is two to a nonnegative integer and hence is never zero.',one='C:For cyclic length one all FFT calls take their identity base branch and the square-root scale is one; linear lengths one and one give the same single product.',zero='C:A zero operand gives zero exact convolution; on successful execution all corresponding products and rounded zero scalings remain zero.',degenerate='C:Invalid lengths, controls or ranges return status; a failed phase or recursion subcall is not reinterpreted as convolution output.',endpoints='C:The linear output is restricted to indices zero through n+m-2 after padding to at least n+m-1, so no trailing circular coefficients are returned.')
add('alg-na-computed-unitary-radix-two-fft','The unitary radix-two butterfly recursion','N equal to two to a nonnegative exponent, a complex vector and bounded phase/recursion controls','a forward/inverse candidate and twiddle certificates or a first indexed failure','Even/odd splitting, increasing butterfly index and conjugation define every operation; no pivot or phase is selected arbitrarily.',empty='N:A power-of-two length with nonnegative exponent is at least one, so no length-zero DFT is invoked.',one='C:The forward branch returns x_0 at length one; conjugating before and after this branch also gives the inverse length-one identity.',zero='C:With zero input and successful phase calls each butterfly product, sum and real scaling is exactly zero; the algorithm still reports independent phase/budget failures.',degenerate='C:At positive depth a zero remaining depth cap returns subdivision-cap-exhausted; invalid arithmetic and unavailable phases return their own statuses.',endpoints='C:Both butterfly positions k and k+N/2 are written for every k below N/2, covering the first and last output indices once.')
add('def-na-dct-ii-and-dst-i-even-odd-extension','Fixed DCT-II and DST-I extension conventions','positive length real vectors with the displayed cosine or sine indices','explicit even and odd extensions with separate endpoint normalisations','Every extension entry is an explicit copy, sign change or zero, and every transform coordinate is a finite sum.',one='C:At length one the DCT-II is the identity scalar and the DST-I factor is sin(pi/2), also one; their extension formulas remain defined.',zero='C:All-zero data give zero cosine/sine sums and zero copied extensions.',degenerate='N:The extension definitions impose no nonsingularity or rank assumption; zero/linearly dependent input data do not create a distinct undefined branch.',endpoints='C:The odd extension explicitly sets positions zero and N+1 to zero; the even extension pairs j with 2N-1-j without an extra endpoint value.')
add('ex-na-prime-length-five-bluestein-transform','The five-point constant-data Bluestein example','the fixed length-five vector of ones and padding length sixteen','DFT value five at zero and zero at the other four indices','Padding sixteen is the least power of two above nine; the root-of-unity sum is explicitly evaluated.',empty='N:This example fixes five input coordinates and sixteen padded slots, so an empty input is not part of its calculation.',one='N:The worked prime length is five; it does not make a singleton-length example claim.',zero='N:The input entries are fixed to one; the zero Fourier coordinates are proved outputs, not a second zero-input case.',degenerate='N:Exact chirp indexing is the subject; no ill-conditioned solve or floating-point success claim is made.',endpoints='C:The verification distinguishes Fourier index zero from indices one through four and uses the no-aliasing threshold nine for padding sixteen.')
add('ex-na-three-sample-dct-chebyshev-coefficients','Three-node recovery of a quadratic Chebyshev coefficient vector','the fixed polynomial two plus T_1 minus T_2 sampled at three DCT-II nodes','DCT values six, three-halves and minus three-halves recover coefficients two, one and minus one','The polynomial coefficients and all three nodes are fixed; the inverse uses explicit positive factors three and three-halves.',empty='N:Three samples are fixed, so no missing sample vector is admitted.',one='N:The sample count is three; the distinct zero-frequency scaling is not a singleton transform assertion.',zero='N:The coefficient vector is explicitly nonzero; any vanishing sampled value is just a value of that fixed polynomial.',degenerate='N:Coefficient extraction uses the fixed nonzero DCT diagonal factors rather than a rank-deficient interpolation system.',endpoints='C:The verification treats coefficient zero with division by three and both positive coefficient indices with multiplication by two-thirds.')
add('ex-na-two-point-circular-aliasing','The two-point wraparound comparison','two copies of the fixed length-two vector of ones','circular output two,two versus linear output one,two,one and padded output one,two,one,zero','The two lengths and padding four are supplied explicitly; all convolution coefficients are finite arithmetic sums.',empty='N:Both input vectors contain exactly two entries; empty convolution is not represented.',one='N:This comparison concerns two-point circular aliasing, not singleton data.',zero='N:The inputs are fixed ones; the final padded zero is a computed tail coefficient, not a zero-input hypothesis.',degenerate='N:The example compares exact finite sums and does not assume invertibility of a convolution operator.',endpoints='C:The final linear coefficient one wraps to circular index zero at length two; padding four leaves index three zero instead.')
add('lem-na-bluestein-chirp-convolution-factorization','Bluestein chirp factorisation of the unitary DFT','positive transform length and padding at least twice the length minus one','a chirp-modulated cyclic convolution gives every Fourier coordinate','Each chirp is an explicit exponential and each residue representative is uniquely specified; no family of arbitrary phase choices appears.',empty='N:The Statement assumes N at least one, and padding is therefore positive; a zero-length Fourier group is excluded.',one='C:For N one the only chirp indices are zero, their phases equal one and the convolution representation returns the sole input coordinate.',zero='C:All a_j are zero when x is zero, so every cyclic sum and the chirp-demodulated Fourier output is zero.',degenerate='N:The factorisation is an exact identity on arbitrary complex data; it does not require nonzero coordinates or an invertible data matrix.',endpoints='C:The proof bounds every chirp difference k-j between minus(N-1) and N-1, and strict separation below M prevents the two extreme residues from colliding.')
add('lem-na-complex-product-six-operation-roundoff-bound','Six-real-operation complex product error','two supplied complex scalars, unit roundoff at most one sixteenth and successful normal-range intermediates','absolute error at most three u times the two input moduli','The four real products, real difference and real sum are specified, so the estimate makes no noncanonical selection.',empty='N:Both complex operands are supplied scalars; there is no vector-length or empty-index input.',one='N:The kernel always has two scalar operands and six real operations, not a singleton-size parameter.',zero='C:If either scalar is zero all four exact products and both component combinations are zero, matching the zero right side.',degenerate='N:No inverse or nonzero operand is required; the only excluded arithmetic branches are the stated nonnormal/overflow failures.',endpoints='C:The coefficient estimate in Proof2.1 uses u at most one sixteenth, including equality, to bound sqrt(2)(2+u) strictly below three.')
add('lem-na-computed-unitary-butterfly-error-bound','Two-output unitary butterfly error','a unit-modulus exact twiddle, a certified phase error at most one quarter and successful arithmetic with u at most one sixteenth','the pair error is bounded by ten u plus phase error times the input pair norm','The output pair and scaling are the prescribed butterfly values; the argument only bounds those fixed operations.',empty='N:A butterfly has the specified pair of inputs and two outputs; it is not an empty transform.',one='N:There is no length-one butterfly branch in this local two-output statement.',zero='C:When both butterfly inputs vanish, the rounded product, sums and scale vanish and the displayed norm bound has zero right side.',degenerate='N:The local bound permits either input to vanish and assumes only the successful-operation model, not data rank.',endpoints='C:The proof explicitly uses phase error at most one quarter and roundoff at most one sixteenth in the fifteen-fourths and ninety-five-sixty-fourths coefficient bounds.')
add('lem-na-mixed-radix-cooley-tukey-factorization','Strided unitary mixed-radix factorisation','integer factors r and s at least two with N=rs','inner length-s transforms and outer length-r transforms reproduce all N Fourier coordinates','Input/output division with remainder fixes each index pair; twiddle exponentials are explicit.',empty='N:Both radix factors are at least two, so neither strided subsequence nor outer transform has zero length.',one='N:The lemma excludes radix factor one; length-one recursion is handled in the separate dispatcher rather than this factorisation.',zero='C:For zero data all inner and outer finite Fourier sums are zero, so the indexed identity remains valid.',degenerate='N:Coprimality is expressly unnecessary, and no rank condition on the data appears.',endpoints='C:The input indexing u+rv and output indexing k_2+s k_1 each range bijectively over zero through N-1, including both extreme indices.')
add('lem-na-zero-padding-removes-linear-convolution-aliasing','No-aliasing support argument for zero padding','the Given data specify positive sequence lengths and M at least n+m-1','the first n+m-1 cyclic coefficients equal linear convolution and all later cyclic coefficients vanish','Residues are unique standard representatives and the proof enumerates the finitely many supported index pairs.',empty='N:The local Given paragraph explicitly fixes positive lengths n,m; the proof does not supply an empty-sequence extension.',one='C:When both sequences have length one, the sole supported sum index is zero; every later padded cyclic index has no supported pair.',zero='C:A zero input makes every supported product zero; both the linear block and claimed padded tail are zero.',degenerate='N:Only support intervals and modular representatives enter the argument; no matrix rank or inverse condition is imposed.',endpoints='C:The largest supported index sum is n+m-2, strictly below M; index n+m-1 starts the proved zero tail.')
add('thm-na-arbitrary-length-fft-correctness-and-cost','Formal exact-constant arbitrary-length DFT correctness and work','positive length with sufficient radix-two caps in the separately declared exact-constant model','exact unnormalised DFT and arithmetic work of order N log N for N at least two','The least divisor and least power-of-two padding fix the schedule; the proof uses induction on smaller integer lengths.',empty='N:The exact-constant model and induction are stated only for positive N; no zero-length logarithm or transform is used.',one='C:Proof1.1 returns the sole input at N one and the Statement separately gives constant work instead of N log N.',zero='C:The formal exact model evaluates linear transform sums, so zero input yields zero output in every size branch.',degenerate='N:Computed phase/range failures are suppressed only in the explicitly idealised formal model; this theorem does not make a computed-success assertion.',endpoints='C:The cost proof bounds the least Bluestein padding by M below four N and separates the N-one base from positive log-length arithmetic.')
add('thm-na-bluestein-chirp-phase-error-separate-from-fft-error','Separated chirp and convolution perturbations','prime N at least two, power-of-two padding and certified successful chirp products','an absolute unnormalised DFT error with distinct delta and convolution E allowances','Certified phases and candidate convolution arrays are given; the proof applies deterministic norm bounds to them.',empty='N:Prime transform length is at least two and K=2N-1 is positive, excluding empty phase/support arrays.',one='N:The prime-input theorem starts at N two; it does not assert a Bluestein length-one case.',zero='C:Zero x gives zero modulated a and, under the assumed convolution bound, zero convolution error and zero demodulated output error.',degenerate='N:Only successful operations and the stated convolution certificate are assumed; there is no nonsingularity condition on x.',endpoints='C:The finite chirp support has K=2N-1 entries and the proof counts exactly that support norm while allowing the tolerance bound one quarter.')
add('thm-na-certified-unit-circle-phase-enclosure','Soundness of the returned rational-angle phase certificate','an actual certified return of the finite phase routine','complex phase error at most epsilon at most the requested tolerance','The return point is the pair of lower interval endpoints; soundness uses no additional choice of an interior phase.',empty='N:The premise is an actual certified return with two finite coordinate intervals, not an empty result or an inconclusive status.',one='N:This theorem is about a scalar phase certificate rather than a transform length or set cardinality.',zero='C:For q zero the target is one; Proof1.1 encloses its real and imaginary coordinates and Proof2.1 bounds the same complex error.',degenerate='N:An inconclusive or range-failure return is outside the certified-return premise, with no accuracy assertion on that branch.',endpoints='C:Both coordinate errors are bounded by closed interval widths; Proof2.1 retains the non-strict epsilon-at-most-tau acceptance boundary.')
add('thm-na-computed-fft-convolution-error-bound','Conditional computed FFT convolution bound','common power-of-two length and successful transforms/products/scaling','a normwise convolution bound retaining twiddle, product, inverse and scaling errors','All arrays are supplied outputs of a fixed schedule; no support subset or favourable error sign is selected.',empty='N:The radix-two exponent is nonnegative, so the transformed length M is positive; the theorem does not define an empty FFT.',one='C:At M one the transform recurrence has rho zero; the bound still retains the scalar product and final scaling roundoff allowances.',zero='C:If either operand vanishes, the assumed successful FFTs and products vanish; every displayed perturbation term is proportional to the product of input norms.',degenerate='N:The theorem assumes successful arithmetic and bounded twiddle errors; it does not require either convolution operator to be invertible.',endpoints='C:The linear-mode conclusion restricts the cyclic output to its first n+m-1 entries; zero padding identifies these and coordinate restriction cannot increase error norm.')
add('thm-na-computed-unitary-radix-two-fft-error-bound','Global unitary radix-two error recurrence','a successful length two-to-m execution with bounded twiddle certificates','forward and conjugated inverse error factor (1+eta)^m minus one','Even/odd coordinates partition the supplied vector and induction fixes the stage recurrence; there is no arbitrary unitary selection.',empty='N:Length two-to-m is at least one for m nonnegative; the norm recurrence has no empty DFT call.',one='C:Proof1.1 explicitly treats m zero: input is returned unchanged and the error factor is zero.',zero='C:For zero input every successful butterfly stays zero; the bound scales with the zero Euclidean input norm.',degenerate='N:Range failures are excluded by the explicit success premise, and uncontrolled twiddle errors do not receive the O(u log N) shorthand.',endpoints='C:Proof4.1 allows m eta equal to one in the exponential-series estimate, while the m-zero boundary is established in Proof1.1.')
add('thm-na-dct-ii-chebyshev-coefficient-extraction','DCT-II recovery of Chebyshev coefficients','a degree-below-N polynomial sampled at the N cosine half-grid nodes','zero coefficient X_0/N and positive coefficients 2X_k/N','The sample grid and diagonal DCT scaling fix every coefficient; no interpolating polynomial is selected from a family.',one='C:For N one only the constant coefficient exists; the formula gives a_0=X_0 and the positive-index range is empty.',zero='C:The zero polynomial has zero samples and DCT values, giving all coefficients zero by the same divisions.',degenerate='N:The diagonal entries N and N/2 are positive for the admitted positive length; no singular coefficient system is admitted.',endpoints='C:Proof2.1 treats the zero-frequency diagonal N separately from the N/2 scaling for indices one through N-1.')
add('thm-na-dct-ii-orthogonality-inverse-and-dft-extension','DCT-II orthogonality, inverse and even-extension DFT relation','a positive length cosine half-grid matrix','a diagonal Gram matrix with distinct constant-mode weight and an explicit inverse','Finite cosine sums and an explicit right inverse determine the transform inverse; the proof does not choose one among many.',one='C:At N one the cosine matrix is the scalar one and the positive-frequency inverse sum is empty.',zero='C:Zero transform data yield zero under the displayed inverse and zero even extension; no division by data values occurs.',degenerate='N:The Gram diagonal contains only N and N/2, hence is nonsingular at every positive admitted length regardless of input data.',endpoints='C:The proof separates k=l=0 from positive diagonal indices and pairs extension positions j and 2N-1-j up to both array endpoints.')
add('thm-na-dst-i-orthogonality-inverse-and-dft-extension','DST-I orthogonality, inverse and odd-extension DFT relation','positive-length sine matrix with denominator N+1','Gram factor (N+1)/2 and the explicit inverse/odd Fourier extension','All sine coefficients and sign-reflected extension entries are prescribed; uniqueness follows from the scalar Gram identity.',one='C:At N one the sine entry is sin(pi/2)=one and its Gram factor is one, so the inverse remains the scalar identity.',zero='C:The zero data vector gives zero sine sums and zero odd extension including both forced endpoints.',degenerate='N:The Gram scalar (N+1)/2 is positive at each admitted length; data rank is not an invertibility hypothesis.',endpoints='C:The odd extension keeps indices zero and N+1 zero; the paired positive indices one through N yield the stated negative imaginary DFT factor.')
add('thm-na-interval-pi-matches-canonical-fourier-pi','Identification of interval and canonical Fourier pi','the stipulated arctangent-at-one constant and canonical cosine-zero constant','four arctan(one) equals the Fourier circle constant','The constants and arctangent branches are already fixed by definitions; the proof identifies them through a unique limit.',empty='N:The inputs are two named real constants, not an index family that can be empty.',one='N:The literal evaluation at arctangent one is a fixed argument, not a singleton-size parameter.',zero='N:There is no zero-data family in this equality of fixed positive circle constants.',degenerate='N:The proof uses differentiability and continuity of the specified principal arctangent at one, not a singular inverse branch.',endpoints='C:Proof1.2 uses the Abel limit as x increases to one, and Proof2.1 uses continuity at that same endpoint to obtain arctan(one)=pi/4.')
# Hermitian/sparse elimination.
add('alg-certified-hermitian-one-and-two-by-two-ldl-kernel','The certified one/two-by-two Hermitian numerical kernel','pivot size one or two, nonnegative trailing dimension, positive threshold at most one and finite controller budget','stored multiplier/Schur data only after rigorous determinant and threshold guards','The controller uses fixed rational expression trees, row-major modulus checks and a counted finite budget; no unspecified certifier or sign oracle is called.',empty='C:A zero trailing dimension is explicitly admitted; empty column maxima are zero and successful output has empty multiplier and Schur arrays.',endpoints='C:The threshold may equal one but cannot be zero; unresolved comparisons or exhausted controller budget return pivot-inconclusive rather than acceptance.')
add('alg-multifrontal-cholesky-and-sparse-triangular-solves','Ordered scalar fronts and sparse triangular solves','a positive-dimensional Hermitian positive-definite matrix and a supplied ordering','a stored factor or indexed pivot/range status, followed by ordered substitutions','Fronts use original vertex labels, children are processed by increasing pivot position and each matrix entry is assigned once.',empty='N:The input requires n at least one; an empty child contribution or final remainder is a zero array within a nonempty front, not a dimension-zero factorization.',one='C:One positive scalar produces one front, its square-root diagonal and two scalar substitution divisions; its trailing contribution is empty.',zero='N:The zero matrix is not positive definite, so it is outside the input class; zero off-diagonal structural entries are retained as allocated zeros.',degenerate='C:A nonfinite/nonpositive assembled pivot gives invalid-pivot, and failed assembly/factor/solve arithmetic gives range-failure rather than a completed factor.',endpoints='N:No continuous relaxation or tolerance interval is an input to this front procedure; the supplied ordering fixes its first and last pivots.')
add('alg-nested-dissection-ordering-on-regular-grids','Coordinate median nested dissection','positive grid side and dimension two or three','lexicographically ordered children followed by median separators','Lower medians, lexicographic child corners and separator order specify the recursion without arbitrary graph separators.',one='C:At side length one no coordinate has length at least two, so the sole vertex is appended as the terminal separator.',zero='N:Side length zero is excluded by k at least one; no empty coordinate grid is passed to recursion.',degenerate='N:The ordering depends only on coordinate geometry and has no pivot or matrix-nonsingularity requirement.',endpoints='C:The coordinate set includes both zero and k-1 in each axis, with each lower median fixed by the floor formula.')
add('alg-sparse-cholesky-with-invalid-pivot-status','Fixed-pattern scalar Hermitian Cholesky','a supplied positive-definite Hermitian matrix, structural graph and ordering','success factors or an indexed invalid-pivot/range status','Preallocation follows the filled graph; each column and inner accumulation uses increasing indices.',empty='N:The factorization operates on the declared Cholesky positive-dimensional input; an empty sum at the first column is defined as zero rather than an empty matrix branch.',one='C:A positive scalar has an empty inner sum, a positive square-root diagonal and no off-diagonal divisions.',zero='N:The zero matrix is excluded by positive definiteness; structurally absent off-diagonal entries are nevertheless explicitly set to zero.',degenerate='C:A zero/nonpositive or nonfinite computed pivot exits invalid-pivot; failed square root or arithmetic exits range-failure even for a mathematically positive-definite input.',endpoints='N:No real interval parameter is present in the sparse recurrence; its index boundaries are the first and final supplied matrix columns.')
add('alg-symmetric-indefinite-block-ldl-star','Declared-block Hermitian LDL factorization','an exhaustive ordered one/two-position partition and threshold strictly above zero and at most one','exact or certified rounded factors only after block acceptance, otherwise indexed failure','The ordering and block partition are supplied; no automatic existence or selection of a favourable pivot is promised.',endpoints='C:The threshold test includes theta equal to one but excludes zero; comparisons are componentwise and empty trailing maxima are defined as zero.')
add('alg-threshold-partial-pivot-sparse-lu','Threshold sparse LU with deterministic row pivoting','a nonsingular real matrix of positive dimension and positive threshold at most one','stored factors with accumulated row permutation or singular-pivot/range status','When a threshold fails the smallest row attaining the current column maximum is selected; earlier multiplier rows are swapped explicitly.',empty='N:The Definition assumes n at least one and a nonsingular input, so no empty factorization input is declared.',one='C:One nonzero scalar has only its pivot row, no multiplier rows and no trailing updates.',zero='C:A zero active column maximum returns singular-pivot immediately; individual unstored entries are treated as zero.',degenerate='C:The zero-pivot and zero-column-maximum tests are explicit failure guards; arithmetic underflow/overflow returns range-failure.',endpoints='C:Theta one is allowed and requires a maximal pivot; theta zero is excluded before any theta-inverse multiplier estimate.')
add('def-elimination-tree-and-column-reach','Structural column sets and parent-path reach','a supplied Hermitian graph and elimination ordering','column set is pivot union later neighbors and reach follows the parent path','The parent map comes from the fixed elimination forest and path following is functional, not a choice of an ancestor.',empty='C:With no later neighbors the column still contains its pivot, its off-diagonal count is zero and the parent path ends at that root.',one='C:For a one-vertex forest the column/reach contain only that vertex and there is no strict ancestor.',zero='C:A diagonal Hermitian matrix has no off-diagonal edges, so every off-diagonal column count is zero and each reach stops at its vertex.',degenerate='N:Only the graph/ordering is used; numerical pivot singularity is not an assumption of these structural definitions.',endpoints='N:No interval or real-valued endpoint parameter appears in the parent-path definitions.')
add('def-hermitian-structural-graph-adapter','The Hermitian off-diagonal support adapter','a Hermitian complex matrix with fixed row/column indices','a zero-diagonal symmetric real pattern records exactly its off-diagonal support','For each pair of indices the pattern is fixed by the nonzero-entry test and Hermitian conjugate symmetry.',empty='C:For a dimension-zero carrier no index pair is tested and the support pattern is empty; this adapter performs no pivot or division.',one='C:For a one-by-one Hermitian matrix the diagonal pattern entry is zero and there is no off-diagonal graph edge.',zero='C:A zero Hermitian matrix maps to the all-zero pattern; the adapter records support rather than asserting invertibility.',degenerate='N:Hermitian symmetry is the only matrix condition; singular and rank-deficient matrices still have the same well-defined support test.',endpoints='N:This support conversion has finite indices but no real interval or threshold parameter.')
add('ex-nonsingular-indefinite-requires-two-by-two-pivot','The zero-diagonal exchange-matrix pivot example','the fixed two-by-two Hermitian matrix with unit off-diagonals','scalar diagonal pivots fail but the full two-by-two block is nonsingular','The proposed block is the whole given matrix, so no search for an unspecified pivot is used.',empty='N:The example is a fixed matrix of order two, not an empty elimination sequence.',one='C:The one-by-one pivot alternative fails because both diagonal entries are exactly zero; the successful alternative uses the declared two-by-two block.',zero='C:Both diagonal zeros are part of the example; they demonstrate scalar failure without making the full matrix singular.',degenerate='C:The verification computes determinant minus one, so zero diagonal entries do not imply a degenerate two-by-two block.',endpoints='N:No continuous parameter or limiting endpoint is varied in this exchange-matrix calculation.')
add('ex-star-orderings-produce-different-sparse-fill','Leaf-first versus center-first elimination of a star','at least three leaves and explicit positive edge weight one over twice the square root of their count','no leaf-first fill versus a full nonzero leaf clique after center elimination','The two orderings and edge weight are explicitly fixed; no separator minimizer or generic no-cancellation choice is needed.',empty='N:The star has a center and at least three leaves, excluding an empty graph.',one='N:The leaf count is at least three; a singleton star is not used for the asserted fill contrast.',zero='C:The initially missing leaf-to-leaf entries are zero; the center Schur update makes them all minus t squared, which is nonzero.',degenerate='N:The chosen edge weight is strictly positive and the example proves positive definiteness; arbitrary singular star matrices are not covered.',endpoints='N:There is no interval-valued parameter here; the leaf count is an integer at least three and the weight is prescribed.')
add('ex-two-dimensional-poisson-nested-dissection-cost','Five-point Poisson matrix on a square grid','positive side length with matrix four I minus grid adjacency','positive definiteness and the two-dimensional nested-dissection cost instance','The grid labels and median-recursion ordering are supplied; the example does not choose an optimal ordering.',empty='N:The coordinate side length is at least one, so there is always a vertex and the matrix is nonempty.',one='C:At side length one the adjacency is zero and the matrix is the scalar four, with one positive pivot and constant work/storage.',zero='C:Missing grid edges have zero coefficients; these zeros leave the five-point graph a subgraph of the permitted grid rather than violating the positive diagonal.',degenerate='N:The example establishes positive definiteness of the prescribed Dirichlet matrix and does not assert a singular Neumann analogue.',endpoints='N:Grid boundary coordinates are discrete vertices; no continuous parameter endpoint is part of the asymptotic cost claim.')
add('lem-elimination-tree-reach-contains-each-filled-neighbor','Filled neighbors lie on the elimination parent path','a supplied pivot vertex and its elimination forest','later neighbors are ancestors and a child contribution embeds in its parent front','The parent is already fixed by the forest; its successive vertices are followed uniquely.',empty='C:If a pivot has no later neighbor, its ancestor-containment assertion is vacuous and there is no child contribution to embed.',one='C:A lone root has no later neighbor and its structural column is the singleton pivot; containment does not require a nonexistent parent.',zero='C:An edgeless later-neighbor set adds no off-diagonal reach; the proof can stop immediately at a root.',degenerate='N:The statement concerns symbolic fill and parent paths, with no numerical pivot-value or rank condition.',endpoints='N:The containment has no real interval parameter; it quantifies over the supplied finite vertex ordering.')
add('lem-filled-edge-path-characterisation','Characterisation of symbolic fill by earlier-vertex walks','two distinct remaining vertices after a fixed number of eliminations','filled-edge membership is equivalent to an original walk with earlier internal vertices','The proof replaces finite walk segments and deletes finite repetitions; it does not select a path in an arbitrary infinite family.',empty='N:A claimed pair consists of two distinct remaining vertices, so the premise supplies nonempty endpoint data.',one='N:A one-vertex remaining graph contains no distinct endpoint pair to which this equivalence could be applied.',zero='C:At zero eliminated vertices a qualifying walk has no earlier internal vertex, so for distinct endpoints it reduces to an original edge.',degenerate='N:Walks may repeat vertices by definition; the argument does not require a simple or nondegenerate numerical matrix.',endpoints='C:The two endpoints must remain outside the earlier eliminated set; only internal walk entries may belong to it.')
add('lem-hermitian-schur-support-is-contained-in-structural-fill','Hermitian Schur support stays within symbolic fill','a fixed ordering with each used scalar pivot nonzero','Hermitian symmetry persists and every new nonzero off-diagonal lies in the neighbor clique','Updates and cliques are determined by the given ordering and entries; no numerical noncancellation pattern is selected.',empty='C:If a stage has no later neighbors its trailing clique is empty and the update creates no off-diagonal support.',one='C:A scalar pivot with no remaining vertex needs no off-diagonal Schur update; Hermitian diagonal reality is retained.',zero='C:A new off-diagonal can be nonzero only through an old entry or two nonzero pivot couplings; zero couplings cannot create a forbidden edge.',degenerate='N:Zero scalar pivots are excluded by the explicit nonzero-pivot hypothesis, so this support lemma is not an existence theorem for admissible pivots.',endpoints='N:There is no tolerance or real interval parameter in the exact Schur support statement.')
add('lem-nested-dissection-fronts-are-boundary-limited','Median-separator fronts are confined to local boundaries','dimension two or three and a recursion box whose maximum side length is s','later neighbors lie in separator union outer boundary and front size is at most three d times s to the d-minus-one','Median hyperplanes and parent boxes are fixed geometrically; the proof bounds their cardinalities without choosing a separator.',empty='N:The argument starts with a vertex in a separator of a nonempty recursion box; an empty box supplies no such vertex.',one='C:The Statement explicitly sets a terminal one-vertex separator equal to its box; the front contains that vertex and satisfies the stated cardinal bound.',zero='N:The recursion side length is positive for a box containing a vertex; a zero-side box has no front to bound.',degenerate='N:The bound is structural for coordinate grids and is independent of numerical matrix rank or cancellations.',endpoints='C:The outer boundary consists of vertices adjacent to a box, including boxes touching the global grid boundary where there are fewer outside neighbors.')
add('lem-hermitian-block-ldl-kernel-roundoff-and-reconstruction-residual','Certified local LDL multiplier and reconstruction error','a successful one/two-by-two kernel with trailing size t','explicit multiplier, Schur and full stored-multiplier coupling residual bounds','Stored pivot certificates and fixed computed arrays determine every residual; the proof takes their norms rather than selecting favourable errors.',empty='C:At t zero the Statement explicitly sets all residuals and e_M,beta,eta to zero; there is no trailing multiplication.',one='C:The k-one branch uses omega_1=u and a nonzero real pivot, independently of the k-two normalized determinant estimates.',zero='C:Zero coupling B produces zero multiplier error and removes its quadratic Schur contribution; exact zero intermediates remain allowed on successful calls.',degenerate='N:Only accepted nonsingular pivots are covered; the coefficient dependence on inverse pivot and normalized determinant is retained, so no near-singular uniform smallness claim is made.',endpoints='C:The strict normalized determinant margin mu below one prevents zero certificate denominators; an equality at the uncertified determinant threshold is not a successful pivot.')
add('thm-block-ldl-star-residual-with-actual-growth','Propagation of block LDL residuals with actual transformation growth','a successful declared pivot sequence and the stated local residual equations','global residual is a sum of conjugated local residuals, bounded with actual amplification','The partition, transformations and their finite products are fixed by the successful run; no uniformly bounded pivot or factor is chosen.',empty='C:For the empty pivot partition the Statement defines both amplification maxima as zero and every stage sum is zero.',one='C:One accepted pivot block has preceding product equal to identity, so the global reconstruction residual is that local block residual.',zero='C:Exact arithmetic has all local errors zero, so both trailing-only and actual stored-factor residual sums vanish.',degenerate='N:The successful-run premise excludes failed or singular pivots, while the bound deliberately keeps inverse-pivot and actual-growth factors for ill-conditioned accepted blocks.',endpoints='N:The theorem has a finite stage index but no real interval parameter or limiting endpoint claim.')
add('thm-multifrontal-elimination-equivalence-and-sparse-costs','Exact multifrontal equivalence and factor/solve arithmetic counts','a successful exact factorization of a positive-definite Hermitian input','front work sums squared front sizes, allocation sums front sizes and solves have linear allocation work','Each entry belongs to the first eliminating endpoint and children are assembled in fixed order; no minimal-fill ordering is selected.',empty='N:A successful factorization of the supplied positive-definite matrix is the premise; empty child contributions do not constitute an empty input claim.',one='C:A single front has d_r one, so allocation, front arithmetic and both scalar solves have constant counts.',zero='C:Structural zero slots remain allocated and are included in each d_r count even when a numerical coefficient cancels.',degenerate='N:The theorem assumes exact successful positive-definite factorization and states no wall-clock or arbitrary-singular-matrix complexity.',endpoints='N:Its sums are over finite pivot fronts rather than an interval-valued analytic parameter.')
add('thm-nested-dissection-grid-work-and-storage-bounds','Grid nested-dissection work and factor storage rates','dimension two or three, a positive-definite grid-subgraph matrix and side k at least two in the rate branch','two-dimensional n-to-three-halves work and n log n storage, versus three-dimensional n-squared and n-to-four-thirds','The coordinate ordering is prescribed, so the proof sums geometric recursion levels without optimizing over separators.',empty='N:The rate branch assumes k at least two, and the separate k-one clause is still nonempty; no zero-vertex grid bound is asserted.',one='C:The Statement explicitly separates k one, with one pivot and constant work and factor storage.',zero='N:Side k zero is not admitted; zero off-grid coefficients are permitted by the structural-subgraph premise and do not introduce a zero-size case.',degenerate='N:The matrix must be positive definite and follow the grid geometry; no singular or arbitrary-graph extension is asserted.',endpoints='N:The theorem distinguishes integer grid sizes, not endpoints of a real relaxation or tolerance interval.')
add('thm-sparse-cholesky-structural-pattern-without-cancellation','Exact Cholesky support inclusion and conditional pattern equality','a positive-definite Hermitian matrix with a supplied ordering','nonzeros lie in fill, and equality needs every permitted numerator nonzero','Cholesky with positive diagonal is unique; the symbolic ordering and recurrence specify the factor without arbitrary pattern choices.',empty='N:The positive-definite Cholesky input is the supplied matrix; an empty neighbor set merely gives a diagonal factor column.',one='C:For a positive scalar there is only the positive square-root diagonal; the strict-lower no-cancellation condition is vacuous.',zero='C:A permitted numerator equal to zero is allowed for the inclusion conclusion but fails the extra hypothesis for exact numerical-pattern equality.',degenerate='N:Singular matrices are outside positive definiteness; cancellation is separately handled by weakening equality to support inclusion.',endpoints='N:The pattern theorem has no interval parameter; it concerns fixed finite row/column positions.')
add('thm-threshold-sparse-lu-residual-with-growth','Threshold sparse LU residual with run-dependent growth','a successful real nonsingular factorization with positive threshold and normal-range operations','entrywise and infinity-norm residual estimates proportional to actual growth','The algorithm fixes smallest-row pivot ties; the residual theorem uses that actual run rather than selecting a small-growth factorization.',empty='N:The Statement assumes n at least one and a positive maximum input magnitude, excluding the empty matrix.',one='C:A single nonzero scalar has no elimination multipliers or trailing Schur updates; the stated positive-dimensional bound remains defined.',zero='N:The zero input matrix violates nonsingularity and M greater than zero; zero individual intermediates are explicitly allowed by the successful-operation premise.',degenerate='N:The theorem covers successful nonsingular inputs and retains actual growth; singular-pivot/range statuses and arbitrary small-growth conclusions are outside it.',endpoints='C:Theta equal to one is admitted and appears in a finite inverse-threshold bound; theta zero is excluded and would invalidate that coefficient.')
# Bidiagonal SVD and strong RRQR.
add('alg-householder-bidiagonalization','Tall-orientation real/complex Householder bidiagonal reduction','a rectangular matrix oriented as A or A-star with p the smaller dimension','bidiagonal work matrix, stored local transformations and rounded candidate residual reports','The phase is first-entry phase or one, zero vectors take identity, and local left/right transformations are appended in order.',empty='C:For p zero the reflector loop is empty and the initialized identity factors give the empty bidiagonal block in its tall embedding.',one='C:For p one only the left reflection is needed; the right-update condition k below p is false.',zero='C:Every zero active column or row uses its explicit identity branch, avoiding division by a zero reflector vector.',degenerate='C:Rank-deficient matrices are allowed; a vanishing active vector uses identity rather than requiring full column rank.',endpoints='C:The final column k=p has no right update, and wide inputs are processed through A-star with the explicitly reversed output orientation.')
add('alg-implicit-shifted-bidiagonal-qr-with-deflation','Finite-budget bidiagonal QR with recorded deflation','a successful reduction record, nonnegative tolerance and nonnegative sweep cap','converged diagonal output or budget/range status with transformation and discarded-entry records','Active blocks are visited in increasing order, shift ties choose the smaller eigenvalue and candidate-value ties retain original index order.',empty='C:An empty bidiagonal work matrix has no nonsingleton active block, so no sweep or modulus readout is needed.',one='C:A singleton active block is skipped and is already diagonal; only its admissible modulus and phase readout are used.',zero='C:A zero leading diagonal triggers the zero-shift rotation without dividing by it; zero annihilation vectors use identity and zero phase factors are not divided.',degenerate='C:Small or zero leading pivots have the stated zero-shift branch; nonconverged blocks remain candidates and carry no accuracy assertion.',endpoints='C:Deflation uses the non-strict tolerance threshold and records each removed old entry; sweep cap zero stops before the first required block sweep.')
add('alg-rank-thresholded-least-squares-by-svd','SVD-thresholded least-squares model and candidate reports','a rectangular matrix, right-hand side, nonnegative threshold and finite bidiagonal-QR budget','exact truncated pseudoinverse model solution or an unverified numerical candidate/inconclusive status','Retained indices are fixed by the strict threshold comparison; the canonical pseudoinverse fixes the minimum-norm output.',empty='C:An empty retained singular-index set gives the zero truncated pseudoinverse; the output still distinguishes original and truncated-model residuals.',one='C:With one singular value the strict comparison either retains its scalar inverse or replaces it by zero; no division occurs when it is discarded.',zero='C:Zero singular values are discarded even at threshold zero, so the retained reciprocals never divide by zero.',degenerate='C:Rank deficiency is handled by truncation; finite-precision counts are unverified unless certified intervals separate every comparison from the threshold.',endpoints='C:A singular value equal to tau is discarded, and when all p values are retained the tail sigma_(p+1) is explicitly defined as zero.')
add('alg-scaled-complex-householder-and-givens-updates','Scaled real/complex reflector and rotation arithmetic','stored active columns or two-entry rotation targets under successful-operation checks','stored local exact-unitary representatives and rounded matrix updates with indexed failures','Scaling maxima, zero/phase branches and prescribed products determine each local operation rather than an arbitrary unitary completion.',empty='N:The local Householder or Givens target has its stated active coordinates; empty outer loops are handled by the calling bidiagonal algorithm.',one='C:A one-coordinate Householder target uses the same phase/norm formula when nonzero, while an all-zero target takes identity.',zero='C:An all-zero active column or annihilation vector takes identity, and exact-zero component results are admitted by the range convention.',degenerate='C:Singular formation data are not silently inverted: zero branches precede scaling/division and unsuccessful range operations return status.',endpoints='C:The local coefficient domains require positive u below one; target-zero overwrites are made only at the actual annihilated coordinate, never an unrelated shift-vector entry.')
add('alg-strong-rank-revealing-qr-with-finite-swaps','Finite-swap strong RRQR with represented-unitary certification','full-column-rank exact input, target k from zero through n-1, f at least one and finite cap','exact success or explicit rank/swap/range status; computed spectral claims need the rigorous score/residual certificate','Greedy norm pivots and violating pairs have fixed tie rules; the lexicographically first maximizing swap is specified.',empty='C:At target k zero the leading block and score index set are empty; the full QR is returned without score or inverse conditions.',one='C:A single selected column is permitted when n exceeds one; with n one only k zero is admitted and the no-swap return applies.',endpoints='C:The target excludes k=n, factor f one is allowed, and cap zero either accepts an already passing criterion or returns swap-budget-exhausted before a violating swap.')
add('cex-classical-column-pivoted-qr-is-not-strong-with-factor-one','Counterexample to the factor-one classical column-pivoted QR claim','the explicit full-rank matrix and target in its Counterexample','a classical greedy pivot output violates the unit coefficient bound','The example fixes the matrix and exact greedy pivots; it does not select a random or generic bad QR factorization.',empty='N:The exhibited selected/trailing blocks are nonempty, so an empty target is not the asserted counterexample.',one='N:The fixed counterexample needs the specified multiple columns; no singleton-matrix violation is claimed.',zero='N:The witness is full rank and has specific nonzero column geometry; a zero matrix is not the bad classical-pivot input.',endpoints='C:The refuted universal bound is exactly factor one; one violating coefficient at the displayed target suffices to refute it.')
add('cor-computed-singular-values-absolute-error-from-backward-stability','Absolute singular-value error from a completed bidiagonal SVD','a successful fully reduced tall-orientation record and represented exact unitary factors','each ordered singular-value error is bounded by reconstruction perturbation plus any rounded modulus readout allowance','The exact represented factors and recorded perturbation are supplied; sorting fixes value order and the argument requires no favourable choice of singular vectors.',empty='C:When p is zero the index range one through p is empty; the per-value assertion is vacuous and the readout branch has no diagonal values to evaluate.',endpoints='N:This is a normwise perturbation bound for ordered indices, with no interval-valued parameter or positive-relative-error endpoint assertion.')
add('ex-absolute-versus-relative-singular-value-error','Absolute versus componentwise-relative error on a diagonal matrix','the fixed diagonal entries one and positive epsilon below one','a normwise perturbation can erase the small singular value while a relative entry perturbation has relative size eta','Both perturbations are specified entrywise in the verification; no optimal perturbation family is selected.',empty='N:The matrix is explicitly two-by-two, so an empty spectrum is not being compared.',one='N:The two unequal singular values are essential to the example; no scalar-matrix generalization is asserted.',zero='N:The small diagonal epsilon is strictly positive; the zero produced by perturbation is an output demonstrating relative error one.',degenerate='C:The normwise perturbation makes the small diagonal zero; this is precisely the rank-loss contrast with a componentwise relative perturbation.')
add('ex-nearly-rank-deficient-design-matrix','Tolerance-dependent rank of a fixed two-column design','epsilon fixed at ten to the minus eight in the upper-triangular witness','exact rank two but numerical rank one or two at the two stated thresholds','The matrix and both tolerances are explicit numerical data; singular-value comparisons are analytic bounds, not a selected sample.',empty='N:The design has two rows and columns and the example uses both singular values.',one='N:Numerical rank one is an output at one tolerance, not a singleton input dimension.',zero='N:Epsilon is fixed positive, so the example does not claim a rank-zero or epsilon-zero input calculation.',degenerate='C:The matrix is nearly rank deficient but determinant epsilon is nonzero; the two threshold comparisons separate numerical and exact rank.')
add('lem-exact-householder-bidiagonal-reduction','Exact bidiagonal-reduction invariant','any real or complex rectangular input with the algorithm tall orientation','unitary left/right factors give the displayed bidiagonal tall embedding','The algorithm fixes reflector phases and identity branches; no arbitrary orthonormal basis extension is required for the reduction invariant.',empty='C:For p zero there are no reduction steps; identity factors give the empty leading bidiagonal block and zero-row padding.',degenerate='C:Zero active vectors take identity as specified by the algorithm, so rank-deficient and zero matrices remain covered by the unitary invariant.',endpoints='C:The right transformation is omitted after the final column, preserving upper bidiagonal form; the wide orientation applies the same invariant to A-star.')
add('lem-implicit-bidiagonal-qr-bulge-chase-unitary-equivalence','Exact one-sweep left/right unitary equivalence','an unreduced bidiagonal active block and its stored left/right rotations','one sweep preserves singular values unless recorded deflation is represented as an explicit additive perturbation','The finite products follow the actual rotation order; deflated coordinates determine the perturbation entrywise.',empty='N:The premise is one sweep on an unreduced active block; a missing block or no-sweep output is not a sweep to analyse.',one='N:A singleton block is skipped by the algorithm and has no bulge chase, so this one-sweep premise does not apply.',zero='C:If an annihilation target vanishes its identity rotation is a unitary factor; any actually deflated entry is recorded with its negative old value.',degenerate='N:The sweep starts on an unreduced block; splitting into singleton or independent blocks is handled by the deflation procedure rather than assumed away in a global convergence claim.',endpoints='C:The finite rotation product restores bidiagonal form when the bulge reaches the final active-block endpoint; the lemma asserts no sweep beyond that boundary.')
add('lem-scaled-complex-householder-and-givens-local-backward-error','Local backward error of scaled real/complex updates','successful arithmetic with positive u below one and each Householder dimension satisfying (r-1)u below one','an exact-unitary update plus explicit perturbation coefficient, including actual target-zero writes','The local representative is fixed by stored reflector/rotation data; the proof bounds the actual overwritten matrix entries.',empty='N:The local theorem concerns a supplied nonempty reflector dimension or two-coordinate Givens update; absent outer updates are not local calls.',endpoints='C:The denominators require strict u below one and strict (r-1)u below one; those singular boundaries are excluded rather than certified.')
add('lem-singular-value-product-bound','Singular-value bounds for left and right matrix products','compatible rectangular matrices and an index within each displayed minimum dimension','a product singular value is bounded by the corresponding input singular value times an operator norm','The proof uses singular-value variational suppliers on fixed finite-dimensional spaces, not a chosen infinite sequence of optimizing subspaces.',empty='C:If a relevant dimension is zero the corresponding displayed singular-index range is empty, so no nonexistent singular value is evaluated.',degenerate='C:No full-rank premise is imposed; a zero product singular value or zero operator norm satisfies the same nonnegative bound.',endpoints='C:The valid indices run from one to the stated minimum dimensions, so the final admissible singular index is included and larger indices are not asserted.')
add('lem-singular-values-lipschitz-in-spectral-norm','Spectral-norm Lipschitz bound for ordered singular values','two equal-shape matrices and indices one through the smaller dimension','each singular-value change is no larger than the perturbation operator norm','The matrices and ordered singular values are fixed; a norm inequality, rather than a choice of vector matching, supplies the estimate.',empty='C:At smaller dimension zero the per-index assertion is vacuous; the formula never calls a zeroth or missing singular value.',one='C:For a scalar matrix the claim is the reverse triangle inequality for absolute values, the one-singular-value instance of the same norm bound.',zero='C:For zero perturbation the two singular lists coincide and both sides of the claimed difference bound are zero.',degenerate='C:Rank changes and repeated or zero singular values are admitted; the bound is absolute and does not divide by a singular value.',endpoints='C:The estimate includes the last singular index p; there is no relative-error conclusion requiring it to be positive.')
add('lem-strong-rrqr-column-swap-volume-ratio','The selected-column swap volume identity','full-column-rank matrix, nonsingular leading QR block and target from one to n-1','the volume ratio equals the combined coefficient/residual score','Selected and replacement columns are supplied indices; Gram determinants and the finite QR identity specify the ratio uniquely.',empty='N:Target k is at least one and below n, so both selected and trailing index sets are nonempty.',one='C:One selected column is permitted when n exceeds one; its Gram determinant is the squared column norm and the same scalar ratio formula applies.',endpoints='C:Targets one and n-1 are admitted, but k zero and k=n are excluded before forming the inverse leading block and a replacement pair.')
add('prop-spectral-numerical-rank-counts-singular-values-over-real-or-complex','Spectral numerical rank as the strict singular-value count','positive matrix dimensions and nonnegative tolerance','numerical rank is the count of singular values strictly above tau','The finite index set is determined by an ordered singular-value list and a strict comparison; no arbitrary rank-attaining approximation is selected in the count.',empty='N:The Statement requires positive m,n, so the singular-value list contains at least one entry even if none exceeds the tolerance.',one='C:A scalar matrix contributes count one exactly when its modulus exceeds tau; otherwise the strict count is zero.',degenerate='C:Zero and repeated singular values are handled by the strict threshold count, so rank deficiency does not invalidate the identity.')
add('thm-bidiagonal-singular-values-relative-under-componentwise-perturbations','Componentwise-relative perturbation of bidiagonal singular values','positive bidiagonal order and epsilon in the half-open interval from zero to one','zero singular-value multiplicity is unchanged and positive values lie between q powers','The perturbation multipliers are given entrywise; finite diagonal-scaling arguments bound them without choosing a numerical algorithm output.',empty='N:The order n is explicitly at least one, so this is not an empty bidiagonal matrix theorem.',degenerate='C:Zero entries must remain zero and the number of zero singular values is preserved; the multiplicative estimate is asserted only for the positive singular values.')
add('thm-bidiagonal-svd-backward-error-under-local-stability','Accumulation of local bidiagonal-SVD backward errors','actual elementary unitary updates and every recorded additive deflation/cleanup change','a global represented-unitary reconstruction with product local-error and propagated additive-discard terms','The actual local transformation lists and discarded entries are given; the proof propagates that concrete finite stage sequence.',endpoints='C:At a final deflation stage the remaining product over later updates is the empty product one; every discarded old entry is still retained in the additive term.')
add('thm-strong-rrqr-singular-value-comparisons','Exact and certified-computed strong RRQR spectral comparisons','full QR exact success or a represented-unitary computed score/residual certificate','leading/trailing singular-value bounds, with reconstruction epsilon retained for computed data','The certificate fixes W and the returned blocks, while each exact score bound is rigorously supplied rather than inferred from rounded tests.',empty='C:For target k zero there are no leading-block scores or inverse conditions; the trailing comparison reduces to the unitary full factor and the stated epsilon allowance.',one='C:One selected column is valid when n is greater than one; with n one only the explicitly handled k-zero branch is admissible.',endpoints='C:The bounds include f equal to one and targets from zero through n-1; neither a target n nor an uncertified rounded score triggers the spectral conclusion.')
add('thm-strong-rrqr-swap-rule-terminates','Finite strong-RRQR swaps from strictly increasing selected volume','full-column-rank input, target below n and f at least one','at most binomial(n,k)-one accepted swaps, with explicit insufficient-cap status','There are finitely many selected subsets and each accepted pair has the algorithm lexicographic rule; no infinite sequence of arbitrary swaps is chosen.',empty='C:The k-zero branch returns success with no swap, so no volume ratio or selected-column inverse is required.',one='C:At k one the finitely many selected subsets are the n columns; if n is one only k zero is allowed and no swap is needed.',endpoints='C:A score strictly above f causes a swap, so equality at f is acceptable even when f is one; a smaller cap can still succeed before any violation remains.')
add('thm-svd-thresholded-least-squares-minimum-norm-model','Minimum-norm least squares for the declared truncated SVD model','an exact SVD and nonnegative strict-retention threshold','a unique minimum-norm model solution with tail and original-residual comparison','The truncated pseudoinverse is canonical once the exact SVD model is supplied; no arbitrary null-space component is appended.',empty='C:An empty retained singular set gives the zero pseudoinverse and minimum-norm model solution zero; the claim remains about the truncated model.',one='C:For a single singular coordinate the model either retains its nonzero reciprocal or discards it; discarded coordinates are zero in the minimum-norm vector.',degenerate='C:Zero singular values and discarded positive values are allowed; original-matrix least-squares optimality is asserted only when every discarded value was zero.')
# Stationary iteration.
add('alg-gauss-seidel-stationary-iteration','Fixed-order Gauss–Seidel updates','positive dimension, matching data, nonzero diagonal and a finite iteration budget','the last lower-triangular solve iterate or an input/pivot failure, without convergence certificate','Least failing diagonal index and increasing-index forward substitution fix all choices; the triangular solution is unique.')
add('alg-jacobi-stationary-iteration','Simultaneous Jacobi diagonal updates','positive dimension, matching data, nonzero diagonal and a finite iteration budget','the last simultaneous iterate or input/pivot failure, without convergence certificate','The diagonal inverse acts coordinatewise on supplied data and the least failing pivot is prescribed; no solution selector is needed.')
add('alg-richardson-stationary-iteration','Finite-budget Richardson residual updates','positive dimension, matching data and a nonzero real relaxation','the explicitly updated iterate or invalid-input/invalid-relaxation status','Each residual update is a fixed matrix-vector expression and the initial iterate is supplied.',empty='N:The input dimension is at least one; a zero budget is explicitly allowed but returns the supplied initial vector rather than an empty-vector input.')
add('alg-sor-stationary-iteration','Finite-budget SOR lower-triangular solves','positive dimension, matching data, nonzero diagonal and nonzero real relaxation','a completed finite iterate or input/relaxation/pivot failure, without a convergence claim','Increasing-index forward substitution is unique after the checks and the least failing diagonal is fixed.',empty='N:The Definition takes n at least one; its empty iteration list at zero budget returns x^(0) and does not define a dimension-zero solve.')
add('def-diagonal-lower-upper-splitting-convention','Signed diagonal/lower/upper matrix splitting','positive matrix order with a fixed coordinate order','the exact identity A=D-L-U, with U=L-star on Hermitian inputs','Each entry is defined by its index inequality, so the splitting is unique and uses no family of choices.',empty='N:The Definition assumes n at least one and fixes coordinates one through n; it does not supply an empty-order convention.')
add('thm-irreducibly-weakly-diagonally-dominant-jacobi-and-gauss-seidel-converge','Convergence from weak row dominance and irreducibility','real positive-order matrix, at least one strict row and strongly connected off-diagonal graph','both exact stationary iterations converge to the unique solution for every right side and start','The proof obtains a positive finite-dimensional weight from its supplier and takes a finite maximum; it does not claim converse graph/row conditions.')
add('thm-sor-converges-iff-a-is-positive-definite-and-zero-less-than-omega-less-than-two','The Hermitian SOR necessary-and-sufficient convergence theorem','positive matrix order, Hermitian positive diagonal and nonzero relaxation','universal-start convergence exactly under positive definiteness and relaxation strictly between zero and two','The proof treats a fixed matrix and uses its eigenpairs/energy sequence; no arbitrary family of solution selections is needed.',empty='N:The Statement assumes n at least one with strictly positive diagonal; the universal-start convergence equivalence is not stated for the empty matrix.')
add('thm-strict-row-diagonal-dominance-implies-jacobi-and-gauss-seidel-convergence','Strict-row-dominance sufficient convergence','a positive-order real matrix with every row strictly diagonally dominant','Jacobi and fixed-order Gauss–Seidel converge for every right side and initial vector','The proof takes finite maxima of row ratios and a component of maximal eigenvector modulus; it does not reconstruct dominance from convergence.')
# Chebyshev interpolation.
add('cor-best-polynomial-approximation-errors-vanish','Decay of best uniform polynomial approximation errors','a fixed continuous function on the closed interval minus one to one','best degree-n errors tend to zero, without arbitrary-node interpolation convergence','For a given positive tolerance the proof uses one polynomial furnished by Weierstrass and then compares all later degree spaces.')
add('ex-chebyshev-interpolation-node-families-and-degree-one-norms','Degree-one root and Lobatto interpolation operator norms','the fixed degree-one root nodes plus/minus inverse square root of two and Lobatto endpoints','operator norms square root of two and one respectively','Cardinal linear polynomials are written explicitly and their absolute-value sums are evaluated on the three specified subintervals.')
add('ex-equispaced-runge-function-interpolation-diverges','Divergence of equispaced interpolation for the fixed Runge rational function','the function one over one plus twenty-five x squared, positive grid degrees at least two and the evaluation points one minus inverse degree','interpolation errors diverge although best polynomial errors vanish','The grid, Lagrange product and evaluation point are explicit for each degree; the proof derives quantitative bounds rather than selecting bad nodes.')
add('ex-exponential-best-error-rate-for-chebyshev-interpolation','Factorial approximation and interpolation rate for the exponential','the fixed exponential function on minus one to one and degrees at least one','a factorial best-error bound combined with logarithmic Lebesgue constants gives uniform interpolation convergence','The Taylor polynomial and both named Chebyshev node families are prescribed; no arbitrary approximating sequence is selected.')
add('lem-cardinal-lagrange-interpolation-and-operator-norm','Chebyshev cardinality, polynomial reproduction and norm identity','either named Chebyshev grid at every nonnegative degree','unique cardinal polynomials reproduce all degree-bounded polynomials and yield the cardinal-sum operator norm','Lagrange cardinal products are explicit; a sign-matching nodal interpolant is a finite construction used for the reverse norm inequality.')
add('lem-chebyshev-cardinal-polynomial-formulas','Off-node formulas for the root and Lobatto cardinal polynomials','degree at least one, angles on the closed half-circle and angle unequal to the selected node','absolute cardinal formulas with the explicit Lobatto endpoint half weights','The chosen node index is fixed in the premise, and the polynomial/product formulas are unique finite identities.')
add('lem-sine-chord-and-lipschitz-bounds','Sine chord lower bound and global Lipschitz estimate','a first angle in the closed interval zero to half pi, and arbitrary real angle pairs','sine lies above its endpoint chord and has Lipschitz constant one','The mean-value theorem supplies one intermediate point for a fixed interval; the argument does not choose points simultaneously for arbitrary families.')
add('thm-chebyshev-interpolation-lebesgue-constant-log-bound','Logarithmic Lebesgue constants for roots and Lobatto nodes','degree at least one with a separately stated degree-zero one-node convention','both interpolation norms are bounded by the displayed universal constant times log(n+1)','A nearest point in each finite angular grid is enough; all distance ranks are then summed explicitly.',one='C:At degree one both angular grids are covered by the cardinal estimates and positive log two denominator; the separate degree-zero clause gives the actual one-node norm one.')
add('thm-chebyshev-interpolation-near-best-error-bound','Lebesgue near-best interpolation error bound','either Chebyshev family, nonnegative degree and a continuous supplied function','interpolation error is at most one plus the operator norm times the best polynomial error','The proof compares every polynomial in the nonempty degree space and takes an infimum, without choosing a best approximant.')
# Midpoint and Simpson cases.
add('ex-convex-composite-midpoint-and-trapezoid-bracketing','Convex midpoint/trapezoid bracketing without smoothness','a Darboux-integrable convex function on a strict interval and positive panel count','midpoint value is below the integral and trapezoid value above it','The reflection, chord and midpoint are explicit panel constructions and all sums use the given uniform grid.',empty='N:The interval is strict and the panel count is positive; neither an empty interval nor an empty panel family is admitted.',degenerate='N:Zero interval length is excluded by a below b; affine and constant functions remain valid convex examples, not an excluded singular case.')
add('ex-cubic-exact-for-composite-simpson','The explicit cubic Simpson equality','the fixed cubic x cubed on zero to two with two panels and unit mesh width','Simpson value four equals the exact integral four','The three nodes and all weights are fixed and the antiderivative is written explicitly.',empty='N:The example fixes the nonempty interval zero to two and three rule nodes; it asserts no empty-grid output.',degenerate='N:The fixed unit panel width is positive, and the cubic has identically zero fourth derivative rather than an undefined remainder.')
add('ex-quadratic-exact-for-composite-simpson','The explicit quadratic Simpson equality','the fixed quadratic x squared on zero to two with two panels and unit mesh width','Simpson value eight-thirds equals its exact integral','The prescribed nodes and weights directly evaluate the rule and the stated antiderivative.',empty='N:The interval and three samples are fixed nonempty data; no empty-grid or absent-integrand case is asserted.',degenerate='N:The unit mesh width is positive; the zero fourth derivative simply gives an exact Simpson remainder for this quadratic.')
add('lem-composite-midpoint-panel-error-kernel','The two-piece midpoint panel Peano identity','a strict interval and a twice continuously differentiable function','nonnegative kernel gives the one-panel identity and cubic-length error constant one twenty-fourth','The midpoint and both quadratic kernel pieces are explicitly defined; compactness supplies a finite second-derivative maximum for the fixed function.',empty='N:The assumption a below b gives a nonempty panel and positive length, so the zero-length interval is outside this lemma.',degenerate='N:The proof requires the stated C-two regularity; a zero second derivative is permitted and makes the kernel remainder zero, while zero panel length is excluded.')
add('lem-simpson-panel-peano-kernel','The two-piece Simpson panel Peano identity','a strict interval and four-times continuously differentiable function','the nonpositive kernel has absolute integral length-to-fifth over two thousand eight hundred eighty','Both quartic kernel pieces are fixed formulas and their midpoint derivative jumps determine the endpoint weights.',empty='N:The interval satisfies a below b, so neither an empty domain nor a zero-width Simpson block is part of the premise.',degenerate='N:The C-four and positive-width hypotheses are explicit; vanishing fourth derivative is allowed and yields exactness, not a missing inverse case.')
add('thm-composite-midpoint-error-bound','Global midpoint error from summing panel kernels','a strict interval, positive panel count and C-two integrand','absolute quadrature error at most interval length times mesh-width squared times the derivative maximum over twenty-four','The uniform nodes determine every panel and the proof sums the finite panel-error bounds; no arbitrary partition is chosen.',empty='N:Positive n and a below b ensure at least one positive-width panel; n zero would make the displayed mesh width undefined.',degenerate='N:The bound assumes C-two regularity and nonzero interval length; an affine integrand may have zero error without violating either assumption.')
add('thm-composite-simpson-error-bound','Global Simpson error from paired panels','a strict interval, even panel count at least two and C-four integrand','absolute error at most interval length times mesh-width to fourth times the fourth-derivative maximum over one hundred eighty','Consecutive panel pairs and their fixed Peano kernels give a finite sum; no arbitrary decomposition is needed.',empty='N:Even n at least two ensures at least one complete Simpson block and the strict interval gives positive mesh width.',degenerate='N:Odd/zero panel counts and zero interval length are outside the premise; an integrand with zero fourth derivative is still admitted and has zero bound.')
add('thm-composite-simpson-exact-through-degree-three','Composite Simpson exactness through cubic degree','a strict interval, even positive panel count at least two and four supplied real polynomial coefficients','the integral equals the composite Simpson weighted sum','The polynomial and block grid are supplied; differentiating four times gives zero on every prescribed block.',empty='N:The interval is strict and the even count is at least two; no empty panel family occurs in the exactness argument.',degenerate='N:Coefficients may vanish so degree may drop below three; the fourth derivative still vanishes, whereas a zero-width interval is excluded by a below b.')
